{"name":"Yukon Gold","hex":"#826a21","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Geography of Yukon","blocks":[{"text":"Yukon is in the northwestern corner of Canada and is bordered by Alaska, British Columbia and the Northwest Territories. The sparsely populated territory abounds with natural scenery, snowmelt lakes and perennial white-capped mountains, including many of Canada's highest mountains. The territory's climate is Arctic in territory north of Old Crow, subarctic in the region, between Whitehorse and Old Crow, and humid continental climate south of Whitehorse and in areas close to the British Columbia border. Most of the territory is boreal forest with tundra being the main vegetation zone only in the extreme north and at high elevations."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Yukonwikimap.PNG/330px-Yukonwikimap.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geography_of_Yukon"},{"title":"Klondike Gold Rush","blocks":[{"text":"The Klondike Gold Rush was a migration by an estimated 100,000 prospectors to the Klondike region of Yukon in northwestern Canada, between 1896 and 1898. Gold was discovered there by local miners on August 16, 1896; when news reached Seattle and San Francisco the following year, it triggered a stampede of prospectors. Some became wealthy, but the majority went in vain. It has been immortalized in films, literature, and photographs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/ChilkootPass_steps.jpg/330px-ChilkootPass_steps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Klondike_Gold_Rush"},{"title":"Yukon Gold (TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Yukon Gold was a Canadian reality television series produced by Paperny Entertainment that aired on History Television. The series followed four or five placer mining crews as they searched for gold over the four-month Yukon mining season."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/b/ba/Yukon_Gold_logo.jpg/330px-Yukon_Gold_logo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yukon_Gold_(TV_series)"},{"title":"Yukon","blocks":[{"text":"Yukon is a territory of Canada, bordering British Columbia to the south, the Northwest Territories to the east, the Beaufort Sea to the north, and the U.S. state of Alaska to the west. It is Canada's westernmost and smallest territory by land area. As of the 2026 third quarter estimates Yukon had a population of 50,356, the most of the territories. Whitehorse, the territorial capital, is the largest city in northern Canada."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Flag_of_Yukon.svg/330px-Flag_of_Yukon.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yukon"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Fireweed_Yukon.jpg/500px-Fireweed_Yukon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Fireweed_Yukon.jpg/1280px-Fireweed_Yukon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Fireweed_Yukon.jpg/500px-Fireweed_Yukon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fireweed (Epilobium angustifolium), Yukon's territorial flower[21] and white spruce (Picea glauca) in southern Yukon near the South Klondike Highway.","link":"ht